Limit: an earlier version of this change claimed the falsifiability table caught an age-based hold sweeper. It did not. The row mutated `unresolved()`, which is a reader that releases nothing, and the test only read it back — a hand-written sweeper inside `claim()` passed the suite untouched while the row reported the guard as covered. Found by adversarial review, confirmed by writing the sweeper. The row is against `claim()` now and the test claims again rather than asking `unresolved()` what it thinks.
Limit: the same review found the `attempt < 1` guard had no test at all; removing it left 20 of 20 green, because attempt 0 then looks up attempt -1 and is refused as unchained — a plausible denial with the wrong reason.
Limit: nothing calls the coordinator yet. A turn needs a binding and an attestation, and only an authenticated preflight bind produces those (#638), so `claim` refuses every actor that exists today. That is the activation embargo as a constraint rather than a rule someone follows.
Limit: the sentinel is only as good as the next run. A tree left mutated stays mutated until someone runs the harness again — the repair is automatic, noticing it is needed is not.
Limit: the two counterexample inserts in the ledger test asserted only `toThrow`, so the new NOT NULL column would have satisfied them for the wrong reason; both now supply it.
Ruled-out: keeping the `lifecycle_state === "SETTLED"` conjunct in the retry rule | the schema pairs state with outcome, so it refused nothing the outcome check did not, and the mutation harness proved no test could kill it. A redundant restatement answers "is this checked?" with a yes it has not earned.
Ruled-out: ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN for claimed_at | it cannot add NOT NULL without a default, and a default here is a fabricated claim time on any row that had one.
Ruled-out: amending v22's DDL in place | its checksum derives from that string, so a database that already applied v22 would keep the old shape while schema.sql described the new one — silent divergence, which is worse than a second migration.
Ruled-out: queueing a second claim instead of refusing it | it holds the caller for the length of a turn, which is the stall this design exists to remove; ordering belongs where the message is durable, not in a caller's stack frame.
Ruled-out: "not currently in another unresolved turn" as the retry rule | it permits silently re-running a message that already completed. What makes a retry legal is the previous attempt's outcome, not its liveness.
Ruled-out: making the vitest child asynchronous so the signal handlers can fire | it changes how every row executes to fix the crash path, and the parked copy covers SIGKILL too, which no handler can.
